<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Following Instructions at School</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> First, I Cut the Paper.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Contextualize simple school announcements according to local needs. Text: Simplify textbook content for peers.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can contextualize school announcements using gestures or visuals to highlight key details (e.g., pointing to Friday on a calendar and saying, "The poster event is on Friday." or showing a pair of scissors and saying, "We will make the poster tomorrow."). Can simplify textbook content with basic words and visuals (e.g., pointing to a picture and saying, "This book is about instructions."). Can simplify classroom instructions or textbook content for peers using simple words and visuals (e.g., showing a picture of scissors and paper and saying, "Cut the paper like this." or pointing to a step-by-step poster and saying, "First, cut the paper. Then, glue it on the poster.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification of key concepts related to explaining or simplifying information.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> Teacher shows various classroom objects (e.g., a paper, scissors, glue, pencil, crayon) and asks students to name them. Then, the teacher holds up a picture of a simple craft and asks, "What are we going to make today?" Students recall vocabulary and previous instructions. Teacher encourages responses like, "We are going to make a poster." or "I will make a mask."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> Teacher shows a simple, visual instruction (e.g., "Draw a big circle on the paper."). Teacher first reads it, then demonstrates simplifying it for a peer: pointing to the paper, making a large circle gesture, and saying slowly, "First, draw a BIG circle on the paper. Like this!" Teacher emphasizes pronouncing /p/ in "paper" and /s/ in "scissors" if used. Teacher also models asking for clarification: "What do I need to do next?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If your friend doesn't understand, what can you do? Just speak, or show with your hands?" (Expected: "Show with hands," "Use visuals.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do we use long words or simple words when we explain to a friend?" (Expected: "Simple words.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Following Instructions at School."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, illustrated "school announcement" poster (printed or projected) about an upcoming art project, e.g., "Art Project: Make a Mask! Date: Friday. Bring: Cardboard, String, Scissors, Glue." Next to it, a simplified step-by-step flowchart or image sequence for making a simple mask. The genre is "school announcement" and "instructions."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Mediation: Text: Contextualize simple school announcements according to local needs. Text: Simplify textbook content for peers.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows the announcement and asks: "What is this poster about?" (Expected: "A project," "A mask.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to the flowchart/images: "What is the first thing we need to do to make the mask?" (Expected: "Cut the cardboard.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When is the project?" (Expected: "On Friday.")</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Teacher helps students connect the announcement to the steps. Teacher uses gestures to contextualize, e.g., pointing to "Friday" on a calendar.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation skills using target grammar and vocabulary.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice mediating instructions. Grammar: Plural imperatives ("Let's..."), sequencing words ("first, next, then"), future tense ("I will/am going to make..."). Vocabulary: paper, scissors, glue, cut, paste, draw, poster, mask, cardboard, string, project. Pragmatic: Asking for clarification ("What do I need to do next?"). Sociolinguistic: Collaborating ("Let's..."). Phonemic awareness of "classroom" (class-room, two syllables).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Matching Instructions:</b> Teacher distributes cards with simple instructions (e.g., "Cut the paper," "Paste the decoration," "Draw a face") and other cards with corresponding pictures. Students work individually to match the text to the picture.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Sequencing & Explaining:</b> In pairs, students receive a jumbled set of picture cards that show steps to make a simple item (e.g., a paper airplane, a small card). They arrange the cards in the correct order using sequencing words ("First, next, then"). One student then explains the steps to their partner using simple sentences and gestures, for example, "First, fold the paper. Then, cut the small part." The partner listens and asks, "What do I need to do next?" or "Can you show me?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Team Collaboration:</b> Teacher divides the class into small groups. Each group receives a single image of a simple art project (e.g., "Make a colorful poster for the classroom."). They brainstorm materials (e.g., paper, crayons, glue) and steps. One student volunteers to simplify and explain the first step to the group using "Let's..." and imperatives (e.g., "Let's draw a big flower. First, get a pencil."). Another student explains the next step, ensuring the use of sequencing words.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Following Instructions at School."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Small group presentation/demonstration of their "Classroom How-To Guide." Students will orally mediate the instructions to their peers.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. Students work in their pre-assigned small groups (from previous lessons, where they started creating their "Classroom How-To Guide"). Each group takes out their partially completed "How-To Guide" (e.g., "How to Organize Art Supplies" or "How to Water the Classroom Plants").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. Each group chooses one specific task from their guide (e.g., "How to organize the crayons"). They identify 2-3 key steps.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. Group members practice explaining these steps to each other. One student acts as the "explainer" (mediator), simplifying the written steps from their guide into simple oral instructions, using sequencing words (first, next, then), imperatives ("Put the crayons in the box!"), and gestures or by showing visuals (e.g., pointing to the crayon box). Another student acts as the "listener/doer," trying to follow the instructions and asking for clarification using phrases like, "What do I need to do next?" or "Can you show me?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">4. The teacher circulates, providing support and encouraging the use of target language, especially "Let's..." for collaboration and future tense ("I will organize the pencils.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">5. Each group presents their mediated instructions of one step to another group or to the class, making sure to use simple words, gestures, and visuals to help their peers understand. (e.g., showing a picture of scissors and paper and saying, "Cut the paper like this.").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment focusing on mediation skills and project completion.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students, in their small groups, will present a section of their "Classroom How-To Guide" (Project 1) to the class or to another group. They must orally explain one key step, using simple words, visuals, and gestures, demonstrating their ability to simplify and contextualize information for their peers.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation Checklist/Peer Assessment):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of explanation: Were the instructions easy to understand?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of simple words and visuals/gestures: Did they simplify the content for peers? (e.g., showing scissors and saying, "Cut the paper like this.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (e.g., paper, scissors, glue, cut, paste, draw).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of sequencing words (e.g., "first, next, then").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of imperatives (e.g., "Cut the paper!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation, especially /p/ in "poster" and /s/ in "scissors."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration: Did group members work together effectively and use "Let's..."?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I explain the steps clearly using simple words and my hands/pictures?" (Yes/No/A little)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did my friends understand my instructions?" (Yes/No/A little)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-8 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Learner and teacher reflection on the lesson's effectiveness and future planning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy to explain today? What was difficult?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can I help my friends understand instructions better next time?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What mediation strategies did students use most effectively (gestures, visuals, simple words)?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were students able to use sequencing words and imperatives clearly?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What specific challenges did students face when simplifying instructions? (e.g., vocabulary gaps, structuring sentences).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How can I scaffold future mediation tasks to improve clarity and confidence, especially for students who struggle with verbal explanation?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the unit on "Following Instructions at School" and the project "Classroom How-To Guide." The next unit will build upon these foundational communicative skills, perhaps applying them to a new scenario or theme.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>